From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Nick Randolph <noreply@platform.uno>
Date: Fri, 14 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat: classify GC handles rooting condemned collectible ALCs

A downstream WASM host doing repeated collectible-ALC load/unload cycles
still strands one context per cycle with the forced-unload opt-in
active: the teardown reports NotQuiescent indefinitely, so some root
keeps the condemned contexts' LoaderAllocator witnesses alive. An
exhaustive managed-side investigation (a BFS root tracer over every
non-collectible static, ConditionalWeakTable enumeration, and direct
probes of the interop/RPC tables) found no managed root -- which leaves
exactly the roots managed reflection CANNOT enumerate: raw GCHandles,
thread stacks/registers, and native structures.

This patch adds read-only diagnostics that narrow that down from inside
the runtime, extending the existing InternalGetScoutRetirementStats
surface (unknown kinds keep returning -1, so the addition is invisible
to every existing consumer):

  * sgen_gchandle_foreach_slot_bounded (sgen-gchandles.c) is a new
    read-only, RAW-SLOT-BOUNDED walk over one handle table. It exists
    because sgen_gchandle_iterate cannot serve a synchronous diagnostic:
    that iterator always walks a table to its persistent next_slot
    high-water mark -- which sgen_gchandle_free never lowers, since
    freeing only NULLs a slot and refills reuse holes -- and its
    callback cannot stop it. The new entry point clamps the raw slot
    range, reports the mark it was eligible to cover, and writes no slot
    at all (its callback returns nothing), so there is no self-store and
    no dislink protocol update either.
  * sgen_scan_handles_into_managers (sgen-mono.c) drives that walk and
    reports, per handle, whether the handle's TARGET is attributable to
    the condemned set. ONE budget is spent across all four type passes,
    so total cost is a fixed number of slot loads regardless of the
    tables' history.
  * assembly-load-context.c builds the condemned set (every collectible
    + unloading context's primary manager plus each shared generic
    manager it owns) and exposes thirteen kinds:
      kind 11 -- strong (Normal) handles, CLASS-attributed   [rooting]
      kind 12 -- pinned handles, class-attributed            [rooting]
      kind 13 -- weak (short) handles                  [informational]
      kind 14 -- weak-track-resurrection handles       [informational]
      kind 15 -- condemned memory managers in scope (the denominator;
                 >= kind 8, which counts contexts rather than managers)
      kind 16 -- condemned managers whose LoaderAllocator witness is
                 still alive
      kind 17 -- take a fresh snapshot; returns its monotonic sequence
      kind 18 -- 1 if the handle walk hit its RAW-slot budget, so kinds
                 11-14/19 are lower bounds
      kind 19 -- handles of ANY type whose target IS a condemned
                 context's own managed wrapper or LoaderAllocator
                 witness (IDENTITY-attributed). Counts HANDLES, not
                 distinct objects
      kind 20 -- the latched snapshot's sequence, recomputing NOTHING:
                 the read TOKEN (see ATOMICITY below)
      kind 21 -- raw handle slots the latched walk loaded
      kind 22 -- raw slots that walk was ELIGIBLE to load, i.e. the
                 scanned tables' combined high-water mark
      kind 23 -- SELF-TEST: retake the snapshot under a deliberately
                 tiny raw-slot cap, so the truncation path is reachable
                 without allocating a full budget's worth of handles

WHAT THIS PROVES, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT. Attribution is PER HANDLE and
ONE HOP. A non-zero bucket establishes that a GC handle DIRECTLY TARGETS
an object attributable to the condemned set -- a NECESSARY, not
sufficient, condition for "a GC handle is the holder". All-zero buckets
do NOT establish that no GC handle is involved, because rooting is
transitive: a handle whose immediate target is corelib-classed while its
object graph reaches a condemned instance lands in no bucket. The miss
classes are enumerated in the contract comment (containers such as
object[]/List<object>/Dictionary, the RuntimeType/RuntimeAssembly/
MethodInfo reflection-cache family, delegates, pinned interop buffers,
and anything reached only through native structures or thread
stacks/registers). The sibling regression
ForcedUnload_RootedPointerFreeObjects_BlockNativeFree already
demonstrates that BCL-typed roots block the free, i.e. exactly the shape
class attribution cannot see. These kinds are a triage funnel, never a
proof of absence.

Kind 19 exists because of that limit: this very patch series promotes
the native->managed ALC handle to STRONG while an unload is in flight,
and the managed wrapper is corelib-classed, so class attribution is
blind to the runtime's OWN most important root. Identity attribution
catches it (and each manager's LoaderAllocator witness) and counts it
separately, so the runtime's expected roots stay distinguishable from a
third party's.

Consistency, cost and safety:

  * ATOMICITY -- every gauge is a field of ONE snapshot. Kind 17
    recomputes it (single set build + a single bounded handle walk + the
    witness count); the gauge kinds read the latched result.
    Recomputing per kind would be mutually inconsistent: a context
    reclaimed between two reads yields "every bucket 0 while witnesses
    are live", manufacturing precisely the false signal this surface
    exists to rule out.

    A single process-global latch does NOT by itself make a multi-read
    atomic, though, because the gauge reads carry no generation of their
    own -- and no native thread race is needed to break it. Two LOGICAL
    callers suffice: A takes kind 17 and reads kind 11, yields; B takes
    kind 17; A resumes and reads kind 12 out of B's generation, and now
    holds a tuple that never existed. The read is therefore TOKEN-BOUND:
    take kind 17, read the gauges, read kind 20, and RETRY if it moved.
    Kind 20 is the one kind that recomputes nothing, which is what makes
    it usable as that token.
  * ONE definition of the condemned set. alc_is_condemned and
    alc_foreach_teardown_manager are now the single source of "the
    managers a teardown would free", consumed by the quiescence gate,
    the kind 8 context gauge and the kinds 11-23 snapshot alike -- so
    kind 16 cannot drift from the quantity the gate actually refuses on.
    The shared walk is also NULL-safe on both manager arrays, which the
    hand-copied loops were not: the forced teardown NULLs them, so a
    read-only gauge racing a tombstoned context could abort the runtime.
  * COST -- O(managers + budget). Membership is a pointer-keyed set, so
    the walk does not degrade quadratically as contexts strand (the
    state it exists to explain), and it short-circuits entirely on an
    empty condemned set. The handle walk is bounded on RAW SLOTS rather
    than on classified hits, which is the only bound worth stating: a
    cap on hits would still sweep a long, mostly-holey table end to end,
    once per type pass, while every bucket read 0. Kinds 21/22 publish
    what the walk visited versus what it was eligible to visit, so the
    bound is observable rather than merely asserted.
  * GATING -- the whole region is DISABLE_THREADS-only, and the sgen
    entry points' declarations AND definitions are gated on
    DISABLE_THREADS as well as HAVE_SGEN_GC, so the walk cannot be
    reached on a build where the single-threaded precondition does not
    hold. Because the bounded walk writes nothing, it needs no
    MONO_PERMIT (need (sgen_world_stopped)) of its own -- exclusive
    access comes from the absence of other threads rather than from a
    collection pause. What remained unproven is ASSERTED rather than
    argued in prose: the walk g_asserts that no collection is in
    progress and that it is not re-entered.
  * PURITY -- m_class_get_mem_manager is a pure lookup (generic-instance
    owner, array element-class recursion, or the class's image ALC with
    the default ALC's manager as the dynamic-assembly fallback), so it
    cannot lazily allocate a manager mid-walk; that is what makes
    "read-only" true of the whole operation and not merely of the slots.
    Dereferencing a target's vtable is safe for the same reason the
    forced teardown is: a manager is only freed once its whole witness
    set is dead, so no live object can outlive its own class's manager.

SCOPE: thread-stack/register and native-structure roots are NOT
attributed here, and are not eliminated by empty buckets either (see
above). Attributing a pinning stack slot needs collection-time
instrumentation of sgen's pin path, deliberately left to a follow-up.

Four lifecycle regressions drive the surface end to end.

CondemnedHandleClassification_ReportsHoldingHandleByType uses EXACT
deltas rather than "more than baseline":

  * each of the four buckets moves by exactly +1 for its own handle type
    and exactly +0 for the others, measured across intervals whose only
    content is the GCHandle.Alloc calls under test -- so the buckets are
    proven separated by handle type, not merely non-zero. Kinds 12 and
    14 are exercised with real Pinned / WeakTrackResurrection handles on
    a pointer-free instance, not asserted to stay at zero;
  * kind 19 is proven to count HANDLES, not distinct objects: a second
    strong handle onto the ALREADY-registered managed ALC wrapper moves
    it by exactly +1 and back by exactly -1 on free, while kinds 11-14
    stay flat. An implementation reporting the SIZE of the identity set
    satisfies "identity >= 2" but fails that pair;
  * NEGATIVE CONTROLS: a handle on a plain corelib object moves nothing,
    and -- documenting the blind spot in an assertion -- neither does a
    handle on an object[] that HOLDS a collectible instance;
  * the STRONG handle alone holds the witness: after every other handle
    is freed it still pins the teardown at NotQuiescent, and freeing it
    drains the bucket by exactly one and lets the teardown complete;
  * set-wide gauges are compared with <=, not equality: the GC-driven
    scout completion can retire a stray an earlier test left pending,
    which would make an exact equality order-dependent.

CondemnedSnapshot_MixedGenerationRead_IsRejectedByTheSequenceToken
drives the A/B interleaving deterministically in one thread: an
uninterrupted gauge sweep must leave kind 20 equal to what kind 17
returned, and a sweep with a competing kind 17 spliced into its MIDDLE
must be REJECTED. Reading kind 20 twice must not move the generation, so
a mutant that refreshes there fails too.

CondemnedHandleWalk_SaturationFlag_IsSetWhenTheRawBudgetIsCrossed
exercises the POSITIVE saturation branch, which every other assertion in
the suite requires to be 0: kind 18 must read 1 under the kind 23 cap,
and the truncated counts must not exceed the full-budget ones.

CondemnedHandleWalk_RawSlotBudget_StopsShortOfAHoleyTablesHighWaterMark
builds the shape the bound exists for -- several hundred simultaneously
live strong handles, all then freed, leaving a table whose persistent
high-water mark is far above the cap while nearly every slot is a hole
-- and requires kind 21 to stop at the cap rather than at kind 22.

All four assert both dark configurations off PlatformDetection, never
off another kind of the icall under test: deriving the expectation from
the same call would be self-confirming, since a regression to -1
everywhere would satisfy both dark legs vacuously. A threads-enabled
runtime has the machinery compiled out and must report -1; the
single-threaded build with the opt-in off keeps the surface live and
must honestly report an EMPTY condemned set (0), with the tiny self-test
cap unable to manufacture truncation out of a walk that never ran.
