+ *   - containers: object[] / List<object> / Dictionary<,> / boxes holding app instances;
+ *   - the reflection-cache family: RuntimeType, RuntimeAssembly, MethodInfo/FieldInfo — all
+ *     corelib-classed even when they DESCRIBE collectible metadata (this is where a real
+ *     residual root was already found downstream);
+ *   - delegates: Action/Func are corelib types; the target instance and method are not;
+ *   - pinned byte[]/T[] buffers held for interop (element class corelib);
+ *   - a strong handle placed directly on a LoaderAllocator that kind 16 counts as live, unless
+ *     it happens to be one of the identity objects kind 19 covers;
+ *   - anything reached only through native structures or thread stacks/registers.
+ *
+ * The sibling lifecycle test ForcedUnload_RootedPointerFreeObjects_BlockNativeFree already
+ * demonstrates that BCL-typed roots block the free, i.e. exactly the shape this classification
+ * cannot see. Read these kinds as "which handle classes DIRECTLY hold the set, and how many
+ * witnesses are still live" — a triage funnel, never a proof of absence.
+ *
+ * ATOMICITY, and why the latch alone is not enough. Every gauge is a field of ONE snapshot:
+ * kind 17 recomputes the whole snapshot (set build + one bounded handle walk + witness count)
+ * and returns its monotonic sequence number; the gauge kinds then read that latched result.
+ * Independent per-kind recomputation would be mutually inconsistent — a context reclaimed
+ * between two reads yields "every bucket 0 while witnesses are live", manufacturing precisely
+ * the false signal this exists to rule out.
+ *
+ * But a single process-global latch does NOT by itself make a multi-read atomic, because the
+ * gauge reads carry no generation of their own. Two LOGICAL callers are enough to break it, with
+ * no native thread race at all: caller A takes kind 17 and reads kind 11, yields; caller B takes
+ * kind 17; A resumes and reads kind 12 — out of B's generation. A now holds a tuple that never
+ * existed. The protocol therefore makes the read TOKEN-BOUND:
+ *
+ *   1. kind 17 — refresh, and remember the sequence number it returns;
+ *   2. read the gauges;
+ *   3. kind 20 — read the latched sequence WITHOUT refreshing. If it differs from step 1,
+ *      somebody else refreshed mid-read: DISCARD the tuple and retry from step 1.
+ *
+ * Kind 20 exists solely to be that token: it is the one kind that never recomputes anything, so
+ * a reader can always ask "is what I just read still one generation?" without perturbing it.
